This comprehensive, step-by-step Setup and Deployment Guide will help you install the required environments, handle system dependencies, and run the securedge_pipeline.py script locally on your laptop.

Step 1: Install Python and Verify Environment

Ensure you have Python 3.9 or higher installed on your laptop.
1. Open your terminal (Linux/macOS) or Command Prompt/PowerShell (Windows).
2. Run the following command to check your version:python --version
1. (Note: On some systems, you may need to type python3 --version).


Step 2: Create a Isolated Virtual Environment

Creating a virtual environment ensures that the packages required for SecurEdge-Vision do not conflict with other Python projects on your machine.
1. Navigate to your project folder:cd path/to/your/project/folder
Generate the virtual environment folder (named venv):python -m venv venv
3. Activate the environment:
	• Windows (Command Prompt):venv\Scripts\activate
Windows (PowerShell):.\venv\Scripts\activate
Linux / macOS:source venv/bin/activate
Once activated, your terminal prompt will display (venv) at the beginning of the line.

Step 3: Install Required Dependencies

Upgrade your core package manager (pip) and install the essential libraries required for the pipeline frame processing, vector manipulation, and future UI building blocks.
Run the following compound installation command inside your activated terminal:python -m pip install --upgrade pip
pip install opencv-python numpy ultralytics streamlit

Special Dependency Node: Face Recognition & Dlib Setup

The face-recognition framework uses dlib as its heavy-duty compilation backend. Depending on your Operating System, run the corresponding command:
• Windows Users:
Installing dlib on Windows can sometimes throw C++ compiler errors if Visual Studio tools aren't configured. You can install pre-compiled binaries directly using:pip install CMake
pip install https://github.com
pip install face-recognition
(Note: Replace the URL wheel with one matching your specific Python version if you run 3.11/3.12, or install "Desktop Development with C++" inside Visual Studio Community Installer).Linux (Ubuntu/Debian) Users:sudo apt update
sudo apt install build-essential cmake libopenblas-dev libx11-dev
pip install face-recognition
macOS Users:brew install cmake
pip install face-recognition

Step 4: Save and Deploy the Script

1. Create a new text file named securedge_pipeline.py inside your project folder.
2. Paste the Python pipeline code provided in the previous turn into this file.
3. Save and close the file.

Step 5: Run the Deployment Pipeline Loop

Execute the pipeline locally from your terminal window where the virtual environment is still active:python securedge_pipeline.py

What to expect at runtime:

1. Camera Authorization: Your operating system may prompt you for permission to let Python access your integrated laptop webcam. Grant the permission.
2. Visual Window Stream: A new window titled "SecurEdge-Vision: Local Monitor Frame UI Buffer" will launch, projecting your webcam feed.
3. Array Processing Metrics: The terminal console will begin scrolling rapidly, printing out individual frame calculations, array boundaries tracking, dynamic identity triage markers, and real-time alert changes.
4. Bounding Canvas Visuals: The webcam window display will draw a green, orange, or red active bounding box border around the array canvas depending on the resolved triage tier.

To Exit:

• Click on the webcam visual display window and press the q key on your keyboard.
• Alternatively, switch back to the command terminal window and hit Ctrl + C.
Troubleshooting Edge Cases

• Error: cv2.error: OpenCV(4.x.x) ... (-215:Assertion failed)
	• Reason: The system cannot find or bind to your webcam index 0.
	• Fix: Ensure no other software (like Zoom, Teams, or your browser) is actively locking the camera stream. If you use an external USB plug-in camera, change cv2.VideoCapture(0) to cv2.VideoCapture(1) inside the script logic.
• Error: ModuleNotFoundError: No module named '...'
	• Reason: The scripts are running outside the environment container.
	• Fix: Verify that (venv) is explicitly visible in your command path prompt line before running the execution string.
